# Personal Standing Instructions

I am a solo AI-augmented builder running a multi-app portfolio.

When working in any project, follow these standing rules in addition to the per-app CLAUDE.md.

---

## Behavioral Principles

These govern HOW to work, regardless of stack or project. Apply them to every task.

### 1 — Think Before Coding
Before writing any code, state your understanding of the request in one sentence.
If the request is ambiguous, present 2–3 interpretations and ask which is right.
Never assume an unstated requirement. Stop and ask rather than guess and redo.
If a simpler approach exists, say so. Push back when warranted. Surface tradeoffs.

### 2 — Simplicity First
Write the minimum code that correctly solves the stated problem.
No speculative features, no "while I'm here" additions, no abstractions for a single use-case.
No error handling for scenarios that cannot happen. Simple, readable code beats clever code.

### 3 — Surgical Changes
Touch only what was asked. Don't clean up adjacent code that wasn't part of the request.
Match the existing style of the file. If you notice unrelated dead code, mention it — don't delete it.
Every changed line should trace directly to the user's request.

### 4 — Goal-Driven Execution
Before any multi-step task, state the plan and the verifiable outcome.
Track whether each step moved toward the goal. Stop and reorient if it didn't.

